A Simple Food and Self-Care Routine for Period Days

Period self-care does not need to be expensive or complicated. A few simple habits, including regular meals, hydration, adequate rest, and good menstrual hygiene, can help you maintain your everyday routine.

Start with breakfast. Choose something that gives you energy and keeps you satisfied. Oatmeal with banana and nuts, idli with sambar, vegetable poha, eggs with toast, or yoghurt with fruit can all be simple options.

For lunch, try to combine carbohydrates, protein, and vegetables. Rice with dal and vegetables is an easy choice. Chapati with chickpea curry and greens is another option.

Keep convenient snacks available. Fruit, yoghurt, roasted chickpeas, nuts, seeds, or banana with peanut butter can be useful when you feel hungry between meals.

Do not forget iron-rich foods. Lentils, beans, chickpeas, spinach, tofu, eggs, and meat can contribute iron. If you rely on plant-based sources, pairing them with vitamin C-rich foods can support iron absorption.

Drink water regularly throughout the day. You can also include soups, coconut water, and water-rich fruits as part of your overall fluid intake.

Rest is another important part of self-care. If you are tired, give yourself an opportunity to slow down when possible. Gentle movement may be comfortable for some people, while others may prefer more rest. Listen to your body.

You do not have to completely avoid your favourite foods. Chocolate, desserts, snacks, and comfort foods can be enjoyed as part of a varied diet. Try not to turn your period into a reason to follow unnecessary food restrictions.

Menstrual hygiene should also be simple and practical. Keep sanitary pads available at home and carry a few with you when going to work, college, school, shopping, or travelling.

Change your sanitary pad regularly and choose one that suits your flow and feels comfortable. If you are unsure which product works best for you, you can explore different options and see which fits your routine.

A small emergency period kit can also be useful. Keep a couple of spare pads, tissues, and other personal essentials in a pouch that you can easily carry.

